Online Canteen Food-Ordering System

1. Introduction

A college canteen can use an online food-ordering system to make ordering faster and more convenient for students. Students can view the available food items, place orders, pay online, and receive a notification when their food is ready.


A mobile application is software installed on a smartphone or tablet. It can use device features such as push notifications and, with permission, the camera or location.


A web application runs in a web browser and is accessed through a website. Users generally do not need to install it, and it can work across devices with an internet connection.


A Progressive Web Application (PWA) is a web application that uses modern browser features to provide an app-like experience. Depending on the browser and device, it can be added to the home screen, cache selected content, and support notifications.


2. Comparison of the Three Approaches

Feature	Mobile application	Web application	Progressive Web Application
Installation	Usually installed from an app store	No installation; opened in a browser	Can usually be added to the home screen
Device support	Often requires separate Android and iOS development	Works on many devices with a compatible browser	Works on supported browsers and devices
Updates	Users may need to download app updates	Updates are deployed on the server	Updates are generally delivered through the web
Notifications	Strong support for push notifications	Browser notification support varies	Push notifications are supported on compatible platforms
Development and maintenance	Can require more time and cost	Usually simpler to maintain across devices	Uses web technologies, with some extra setup for PWA features
Offline use	Can be designed for offline features	Usually limited without extra development	Can cache selected resources and provide limited offline access

3. Recommended Approach: Progressive Web Application (PWA)

A PWA is a suitable choice for the college canteen system.


Students can open the ordering system using a link or add it to their phone's home screen. They do not have to install a separate application from an app store. The college can maintain one application for students using different devices, and updates can be made centrally.


A PWA can also provide an app-like experience and send order-status notifications on supported browsers and devices. However, notification and installation support can vary by platform, so the system should also show order status inside the application.


For a single-college canteen, a PWA offers a practical balance of accessibility, development cost, and maintenance.


4. Four Major Functionalities

4.1 Menu Browsing


Display food items with names, prices, descriptions, and availability.

Organize items into categories such as snacks, meals, and beverages.

Allow students to view the latest menu and item availability.


4.2 Order Placement


Allow students to select food items and add them to a cart.

Let students change quantities or remove items before checkout.

Generate an order number and show the order summary.


4.3 Online Payment


Provide a secure payment gateway for supported payment methods.

Confirm an order only after the payment result is verified.

Display payment status and provide a transaction reference where available.


4.4 Order Status and Notifications


Show statuses such as “Received”, “Preparing”, and “Ready for Pickup”.

Notify students when the canteen accepts the order and when it is ready.

Provide an in-app order-status page as a backup if push notifications are unavailable.


5. Three Development Challenges

5.1 Payment Security and Reliability

The system must protect payment-related information and communicate securely with the payment gateway. It must handle failed, delayed, or duplicate payment responses and avoid creating duplicate orders.


5.2 Real-Time Order Updates

The canteen staff and students need to see accurate order statuses. The system must update the status reliably, even when many students place orders during busy breaks.


5.3 Browser and Device Compatibility

PWA features such as installation and push notifications do not behave identically on every browser or device. The development team must test on common student devices and provide an in-app status option when notifications are not supported or permitted.


